    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n1666" root="بأز" body-key="b">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بَأْزٌ</span></b>  <i>i. q.</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بَازٍ</span> [which see in art. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بزو</span>; and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بَازٌ</span>]: pl.
[of pauc.] <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَبْؤُزٌ</span>, and [of mult.] <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بُؤُوزٌ</span> and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بِئْزَانٌ</span>.
(K.) IJ holds that the <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أ</span> is substituted for <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ا</span>, and
that it remains in <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ابؤز</span> and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بئزان</span> like as is the
case in <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَعْيَادٌ</span> [in which the <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ى</span> is substituted for <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">و</span>

and remains in the pl. because it is substituted for
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">و</span> in the sing. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">عِيدٌ</span>]. (TA.)

